                .container
                    h2 Summery
                    h3 RESEARCH HIGHLIGHTS
                    h4 FMN2 Associates with a Perinuclear Membrane, Actin, and FA System that Moves with the Nucleus during Cell Migration
                    p We sought to characterize the role of the formin FMN2 in FA function and cell migration. Immunolocalization analysis of primary mouse embryonic fibroblasts (MEFs)
                        |  plated on fibronectin (FN)-coated coverslips and spinning disk confocal (SDC) microscopy showed FMN2 localization to fibrils underneath the nucleus that partially co-localized with a subset of perinuclear actin bundles and FA (
                        a(href="#")  Fig.1A
                        |  ). 3D reconstructions of SDC z stacks showed that FMN2 localized to the ventral cell surface and foci in the nucleus [4] but was absent from the dorsal cell surface (Fig. 1B). Expression of C-terminally tagged FMN2-GFP (Fig.1C–1G and S1) and imaging with total internal reflection fluorescence (TIRF) microscopy (Fig.1C) confirmed that FMN2 was concentrated at the ventral cell surface in MEFs and was localized similarly in human endothelial and intestinal adenocarcinoma cells, canine kidney epithelial cells, and mouse melanoma cells (Fig.S1 and S7). Immunostaining of FMN2 and microtubules, vimentin intermediate filaments, or the FA formin INF2 revealed no substantial co-localization (
                        a(hre="#")  Fig.S1
                        | ).


